  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\n- A Mainly Rain Event for late Thanksgiving\n\nWe continue to watch the system expected to affect the area over the \nfirst half of the Thanksgiving period. Latest data coming in, \nincluding some 12z data continues to point to a mostly rain event \nfrom late Thanksgiving into Friday.\n\nDeep southern stream trough over the Southern Plains and heading \ntoward the Gulf Coast will help to induce sfc low development SW of \nthe Florida Keys. This low will be rotated along the Eastern \nSeaboard and back toward Michigan by Thursday night as the upper low \ncomes in from the NW and phases with the Gulf Trough. \n\nNormally this phasing late November would produce a snowstorm with \nplenty of Gulf moisture available. The problem with this thinking \nthis time is there is a significant lack of cold air involved. The \nupper low diving SE does not have real cold air aloft to start. Then \nit drops down the front side of the upper ridge, and the coldest \nof the air (-6C of so) modifies. \n\nThe air moving in with the rain from the SSE will be plenty warm for \np-type to easily be rain here with 850 mb temperatures +3 to +4C. We \ndo see the cold pool aloft rotate in here late Thursday night/Friday \nmorning. 850 mb temps are expected to drop to around -3C or so. \nModels have been generally showing the trend of the higher \nelevations of Central Lower and the Irish Hills toward Jackson \nseeing the best chance for snow with the melting layer being the \nmost shallow at those locations. Forecast sfc temps and dew points \nwould support mostly rain also. Can't rule out some light grassy \naccumulations in the aforementioned areas.\n\nAny snow that mixes in early Fri will trend toward rain again as the \nprecipitation is moving out on Friday as temperatures aloft begin to \nmoderate as the cold pool aloft moves east.\n\n- Likely Rain Event Sunday, but needs to be watched\n\nWe see an upper ridge pass through Friday night, before a weak short \nwave trough moves through on Saturday. The short wave trough should \npass through dry as the atmosphere remains fairly dry in the wake of \nthe Thu/Fri system sweeping out the moisture.\n\nWe see another southern stream system then approach the region again \non Sunday. This system will come out of the Plains, and advect warm \nand moist air up toward the area ahead of it. Cold air is not far \nbehind this system, but for right now it is not quite close enough \nto bring the p-type as snow. It looks to be mainly rain again.\n\nEven though it looks like more rain right now, the northern stream \nand associated cold air are not far away. It is close enough, that \nif the southern stream slowed down some, better phasing could occur \nand p-type could trend toward more snow. A lot of uncertainty exists \nat this point, so no definitive answer is set yet. This will need to \nbe watched with it coming likely on Sunday, when many people will be \nheading back home from Holiday travel.\n\nOnce this system moves out by Monday, we should be left with dry \nweather and seasonable temperatures by late November standards.",
